This is the legally conservative default: it reserves all rights and grants
none. That is safe, but it is not yet usable — a client library that grants a
user no rights gives them no permission to install or run it, which is not the
intent of publishing an SDK.

Before release, replace this file with terms that state at minimum:

  - what a user MAY do (install, use, and redistribute the client in order to
    call the Litica service)
  - whether modification and forking are permitted
  - that the terms cover this client only, not the Litica service itself
  - warranty disclaimer and limitation of liability
  - governing law

Many commercial SDKs are published under a permissive open-source licence (MIT
or Apache-2.0) even when the backing service is closed, precisely because the
client contains no proprietary logic — it only makes HTTP calls. That is worth
considering, but it is a decision for Litica, Inc. and its counsel, not a
default to be assumed here.

Until this file is replaced, this software is provided for evaluation by
Litica, Inc. and its authorised partners only.
